## 2026-09-25 (publisher docs)
- Expanded README's "Publisher: core sample code" section. Kept the
  minimal example, added an "Every builder parameter" subsection that
  sets all 13 builder options (grouped: connection / TLS+mTLS /
  publisher-scope QoS defaults, with each default in a comment), shows
  all 6 publish forms, and is followed by a builder reference table
  (method, default, notes) plus the 8 Priority and 2 CongestionControl
  values.
- Verification approach worth repeating: I did NOT hand-verify the API
  surface from reading the source. I wrote the README example out to a
  scratch .java file and compiled it against `target/classes` with
  JDK 21. Exit 0, so every method name, signature and overload in the
  README provably exists. Cheap, and it catches the doc-drift class of
  bug that the javadoc workflow breakage was an instance of.
- Fixed a real doc inaccuracy found while doing this: `publishString`
  in `PureJavaZenohPublisher.java` was documented as publishing "with
  the Zenoh string encoding tag", but it delegates to
  `publish(subKey, bytes)` which passes `Encoding.EMPTY` (id 0, and
  the Encoding field is omitted from the wire entirely). It never set
  `ID_ZENOH_STRING`. Rewrote the javadoc to state what it actually
  does: UTF-8 bytes, no encoding tag, subscribers must know to decode
  as UTF-8. Chose to fix the DOC rather than the behaviour, because
  changing it to emit ID_ZENOH_STRING would alter the wire output and
  break the "byte-identical to the pre-QoS client" property the whole
  class is careful to preserve. If a future session wants a real
  encoding tag, add a new overload, do not change this one.
- Also removed the last em-dash in README.md (line 86, mTLS section),
  per the global documentation style rule.
- Verified with `mvn -q -o package` (exit 0) and a javadoc run (exit 0,
  no new warnings on the edited class).

## 2026-09-25 (javadoc CI fix)
- Note on dates: the three entries below labelled `2026-09-26` actually
  happened on 2026-09-25 (confirmed against `git log --date=short`).
